  "reason": "1) Evidence/uncertainty: No experiments have been completed, so there is zero information about which modality, encoder, profile setting, or aggregation strategy is most effective. The entire design space is unexplored. 2) What this tests: config_001 (rna_only, singleprofile, pca_n2000) establishes the simplest possible baseline—a single-modality, single-profile model with a standard linear dimensionality reduction. It answers the foundational question: how well does a minimal RNA-only model perform, and what is the floor of the search space? 3) Why it is the best first experiment: With no prior evidence, the most efficient use of the first experiment is to anchor the search with a fast, interpretable, low-complexity baseline. This gives a clear reference point against which all subsequent experiments (other encoders, multiprofile, image-only, multimodal, different aggregations/fusions) can be meaningfully compared. It also trains quickly, reducing the risk that the first experiment is uninformative or fails. Starting here avoids the trap of jumping to a complex multimodal configuration whose performance cannot be attributed to any specific design choice without a simple reference."
